<sn><md>### Example

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**local** {.text-center}
```bash
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd Documents
          -1 2026-09-01 09:14:37        -1 Books
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd Documents/Books
          -1 2026-09-01 09:15:19        -1 Notes
```
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**Remote** {.text-center}
```bash
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd gd:
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 BCS
          -1 2026-06-10 10:00:15        -1 Mobile
          -1 2026-09-01 09:23:55        -1 Notes
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd gd:BCS
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:55        -1 Bangla Grammer
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 Bangla Literature
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 Bangladesh  Affairs
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:55        -1 Computer and ICT
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 English Grammar
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 English Literature
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 General Science
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 Geography
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:55        -1 International Affairs
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 QNA
          -1 2026-08-20 07:02:54        -1 math

```
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Important: `lsd` Is Not Recursive

Suppose your Drive contains:

```text
gdrive:
├── Documents
│   ├── University
│   │   └── Notes
│   └── Personal
└── Photos
```

Running:

```bash
rclone lsd gdrive:
```

will show only:

```text
Documents
Photos
```

It will **not** automatically display `University`, `Notes`, or other subdirectories.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Making `lsd` Recursive

Just add `-R` flag with `lsd` command.


:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**local** {.text-center}
```bash
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd -R Documents/
          -1 2026-09-01 09:14:37        -1 Books
          -1 2026-09-01 09:15:19        -1 Books/Notes
```
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**Remote** {.text-center}
```bash
emon@emon:~$ rclone lsd -R gd:Notes
          -1 2026-09-01 09:24:24        -1 FolderA
          -1 2026-09-01 09:24:38        -1 FolderA/FolderB

```
:::
:::

</md></sn><sn><md>### Summary

**rclone lsd** 

- syntax : `rclone lsd path`
- show lists of dir to a given path
- not recursive by default,use `-R` flag to make it recursive
- format is 
  ```
  DIRECTORY_ID    MODIFICATION_TIME    DIRECTORY_NAME
  ```  </md></sn>